Distilled SED Baseline
Credit and motivation
I shared a simple Perch distilled SED model starter notebook. It is entirely motivated by the one and only hengk. Please refer to his original comment here, I just implemented it in my existing pipeline. Show him some love for all that he shares on Kaggle for everyone to learn from.

Idea
The backbone learns to reproduce Perch v2's 1536-d embeddings via MSE distillation — inheriting representation quality from a model trained on 14,795 species. A separate SED classification head trains on the backbone's detached features (stop-gradient), so the two losses never fight over the backbone. At inference the distillation head is thrown away.

Here is the architecture: 

There are some obvious architecture experiments to try — the distillation head projects B0's 1280-d features up to Perch's 1536-d space (you could use a larger backbone, or meet in the middle with a shared 512-d space), the 512-d bottleneck in the SED head is arbitrary, swap GeM for LSE pooling, etc.

Results
Comparing the same model with and without distillation provides a consistent +0.02 leaderboard boost across my submissions so far.. The shared notebook uses no metadata priors, TTA, post processing, or clips longer than 5s. It can be improved to achieve single model scores above 0.93 on the public leaderboard.

Notes
Please note that this model is tediously slow to train on Kaggle as is and will likely require some adjustments to train. I ported it over from my Colab enviroment with the help of Claude. I just wanted to share as much of the training code as possible for those that are interested in trying it themselves. It takes about 100s an epoch to run on the Colab G4 GPU.

If you want to dramatically lower the training time/computation demands, you can preprocess the Perch embeddings once and just load them in instead of running Perch inference every batch.

I hope this is a useful resource to my fellow Kagglers and the competition hosts. Good luck everyone.

