Open Ended Questions
What is the role of augmentation in feature-space knowledge distillation? The role of augmentation is well-understood in standard Hinton-style KD and Noisy Student where the asymmetry forces the student to learn invariance to the augmentation. But for feature-space distillation against a frozen teacher, best practices are not obvious.

If you align augmentations between teacher and student, augmentations serve only as training input diversity. If you show the student augmented data but the teacher clean data, you're asking the student to learn invariance to that augmentation. The viability of the second framing depends on whether the augmentation preserves underlying signal — destructive augmentations like SpecAugment break this framing because there's no underlying clean signal for the student to recover at the masked locations. Can we exploit signal-preserving augmentation asymmetry while keeping destructive augmentations aligned (or absent)?

The unanswered questions: Under what conditions does augmentation asymmetry help feature distillation? Are signal-preserving asymmetries (e.g., student-only gain perturbation against clean teacher) genuinely useful for learning invariance, or does the same input-diversity argument favor aligned application? Are there augmentation classes beyond the obvious ones (SpecAugment, gain, noise, mixup) that have different alignment characteristics worth exploring?


